MWANGAZA HARDWARE — VISUAL DESIGN STANDARD

1. Purpose

Establish a consistent, professional, accessible and fast visual experience across every Mwangaza Hardware website page.

2. Design principles

Professional, practical and trustworthy.

Mobile-first and responsive.

Clear product information and prominent customer actions.

Authentic construction and hardware imagery.

Consistent layouts, typography, spacing, colors and components.

No decorative effect may compromise readability, performance or usability.

3. Branding

Use one owner-approved logo.

Define and document the approved color palette and typography before implementation.

Use consistent spacing, border radii, buttons and form controls.

Maintain sufficient text/background contrast.

Do not invent official branding or imply unapproved affiliations.

4. Photography and graphics

Use relevant, high-quality images with appropriate usage rights.

Keep product photographs consistent in proportions and presentation.

Use optimized image formats and appropriate dimensions.

Provide meaningful alternative text for informative images.

Use empty or fallback states when images fail to load.

Inspect AI-generated images for inaccurate products, distorted text, unsafe demonstrations and misleading claims.

Never present a fabricated product photograph as a verified photograph of actual store stock.

5. Page requirements

Home

Clear value proposition, product discovery, key categories, useful customer actions and verified contact options.

Catalogue

Consistent product cards, readable names, units and prices, useful filters, and clear handling of unavailable information.

Materials calculator

Clear inputs, visible quantities and unit prices, readable totals, validation messages and a mobile-friendly estimate summary.

DIY Learning Centre

Readable educational layouts, relevant instructional images, clear steps and visible safety guidance.

Help, About and Contact

Simple navigation, verified business information, accessible forms and functional contact links.

6. Responsive design

Design for narrow phone screens first.

Prevent horizontal overflow.

Ensure forms and buttons are usable by touch.

Test common Android viewport sizes and browsers.

Check larger screens without compromising the mobile layout.

Ensure important content remains usable if decorative images or animations fail.

7. Performance

Compress and appropriately size images.

Lazy-load noncritical images.

Avoid unnecessary video backgrounds and heavy animations.

Minimize third-party scripts and excessive dependencies.

Reserve space for images to reduce layout shifts.

Test page performance after significant visual changes.

8. Accessibility

Provide readable text and adequate contrast.

Label form controls clearly.

Provide useful alternative text for informative images.

Do not use color alone to convey meaning.

Support keyboard navigation where applicable.

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

Ensure error, loading and success states are understandable.

9. Reusable components

Create shared components for navigation, buttons, product cards, form fields, notices, pricing displays and page footers where appropriate.

Do not duplicate styling unnecessarily across pages.

10. Visual QA gate

Before a page is accepted:

Compare it against the approved design direction.

Inspect the mobile layout.

Check image quality, cropping and loading.

Verify typography, spacing, contrast and alignment.

Test every visible button, link and form.

Check empty, loading, error and success states.

Run applicable accessibility and performance checks.

Correct defects and retest.

Record unresolved limitations.

11. Approval and changes

The authorized project owner approves the final logo, brand direction and business-facing visual assets.

Significant visual changes must be tested and reviewed before publication.

A page is not complete merely because it looks attractive in a screenshot. It must also function correctly, load efficiently and remain accessible.

12. Definition of done

A visual change is complete when the implementation matches the approved design, works on relevant devices, passes applicable checks and has no known unresolved release-blocking defects.
